WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.440
Some of the best films are subjective, meaning
their stories can be interpreted a number

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.360
of different ways depending on the viewer's
own experiences.

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.280
But an excessively ambiguous ending or a sudden
shift in tone can leave audiences scratching

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:13.500
their heads in confusion.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:17.560
Don't worry — we're here to explain some
of the most baffling movie endings that don't

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:19.460
mean exactly what you think they do.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:22.400
Beware, however: There are spoilers ahead.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.140
In Bruges

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:29.720
In this darkly comic morality tale, assassins
Ray and Ken are sent to Bruges to lay low

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:31.040
after a hit gone wrong.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.790
By the end of the film, half the cast is dead,
and Ray is being carried away to an ambulance,

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:38.020
pondering the nature of mortality — and
Hell.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:42.960
Viewers are ultimately left to wonder whether
or not he survived the film's bloody climax.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.120
In one reading of the film, however, this
question doesn’t matter, because Ray is

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:49.580
— wait for it — already in Purgatory.

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:54.820
Earlier in the film, Ken takes Ray on an arts
tour of Bruges, whereupon the two view Hieronymus

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:56.680
Bosch's "The Last Judgement" painting.

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:01.180
It's the one segment of the tour that Ray
actually seems invested in, and it sparks

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:04.659
a conversation about Purgatory, a realm which
Ray describes as …

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:06.820
"kinda like in betweeney one.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:10.940
You're not really f-----, but you weren't
all that great either.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:15.820
The final scene of the movie mirrors Bosch's
painting, as a fatally wounded Ray looks up

00:01:15.829 --> 00:01:20.959
at mysterious animal-faced creatures — really
just actors in costume — while he wonders

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:22.400
what punishments await him.

00:01:22.649 --> 00:01:27.359
He thinks Bruges is Hell, but maybe it's just
a place for people not quite bad enough for

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:28.220
the real thing.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:29.920
Total Recall

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:36.160
1990's mind-bending Total Recall has become
a cult classic in the decades since its release.

00:01:36.169 --> 00:01:41.139
Adapted from sci-fi author Philip K. Dick's
short story "We Can Remember It for You Wholesale,"

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:45.599
the film centers around Douglas Quaid, a construction
worker from the year 2084.

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:51.479
Though, Quaid may or may not also be a secret
agent with memory loss… and a rebel involved

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:52.700
in a war on Mars.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:57.220
The film continually encourages audiences
to question the reality of what they're watching.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:02.340
Are you actually seeing Quaid finish his secret
mission, or is this part of the Recall procedure?

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:06.420
One moment near the end of the film seems
to suggest most of the film took place in

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:06.920
his noggin.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:12.060
Early in the movie, before the Recall procedure
has started, a lab tech reading Quaid's dreams

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.860
notes that they include "a blue sky on Mars."

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:18.780
While the similarities between the character
Melina and the woman in Quaid's dreams can

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:23.220
be explained away by his buried memories,
the lab techs are clearly surprised by his

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.220
conception of a Mars with a blue sky.

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:29.620
When the movie ends with Quaid and Melina
looking over the Martian sky, newly blue from

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:33.909
oxygen after saving the planet, it's the biggest
clue to the viewer that they're watching a

00:02:33.909 --> 00:02:34.409
dream.

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:35.820
Get Out

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:41.060
The story of Chris, a black man meeting his
white girlfriend's parents for the first time,

00:02:41.069 --> 00:02:45.859
Get Out borders on dark comedy at times — but
the genre-bending flick goes full horror when

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:47.960
the family's chilling secret comes to light.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.730
By the end of the movie, Chris has escaped
the grounds of the Armitage mansion with his

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:56.500
friend Rod after killing the family and setting
their secret brain-swapping laboratory ablaze.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:00.640
The end of the movie sees Rod telling Chris
that he can "Consider this situation ''

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:01.140
handled."

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:05.819
But Chris doesn't respond to Rod — instead,
he stares dejectedly into the dashboard as

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:09.800
they drive away, leaving his girlfriend, Rose,
to die in the street behind them.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:13.840
While it's cathartic to see the hero of the
film survive, defeat the villains, and drive

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:18.700
off with his friend, the ending is a bit darker
than people realize: Chris knows the situation

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:19.480
isn't "handled."

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:23.610
Logan, a victim of the procedure shown earlier
in the film, is still being mind-controlled,

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:28.260
and a few dozen old, white gentry are still
stealing black bodies and minds.

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:32.540
In the alternate ending, these tragic realities
are made much more explicit.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:36.340
Chris winds up in jail for the murder of the
Armitage family, with no way to prove his

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:38.300
innocence or expose their crimes.

00:03:38.609 --> 00:03:42.180
He tells Rod that at least he "stopped it"
as he's led to his cell, where he's surrounded

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:43.300
by other black men.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:49.000
In both endings, racism isn't vanquished just
because Chris is able to defeat the rich body-snatchers;

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:53.760
they were just one particularly repugnant
example of a systemic—and very real—horror.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:55.260
Fargo

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:00.080
Fargo ranks among the most straightforward
of the Coen brothers' films; it's the story

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:04.310
of a small town crime, a greedy little man
with a massive ego, and a witty sheriff with

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:04.900
a good heart.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:09.719
Still, viewers are more likely to remember
Steve Buscemi's performance — and his gruesome

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:13.319
disposal in the wood chipper — than the
movie's quiet, touching ending.

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:17.540
In the film's final scene, Sheriff Marge is
shown laying in bed with her husband Norm,

00:04:17.549 --> 00:04:22.000
who laments that his mallard painting was
passed over for the first-class stamp contest.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:23.520
Margie says she proud of him, adding …

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.440
"Heck Normie you know we're doing pretty good."

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:33.780
The subtle ending completely encapsulates
the core themes of the movie, refuting the

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:36.180
naked greed of nearly every other character.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:41.440
From Jerry, whose ambitions lead to the deaths
of nearly half a dozen people, to Mike's desperate

00:04:41.449 --> 00:04:45.360
desire for Marge to believe that his life
is more interesting — and tragic — than

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:49.720
it seems, the characters in Fargo sow chaos
when they focus on what they want rather than

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:50.520
what they have.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:54.440
Marge's confirmation of the good life she
and her husband have built together changes

00:04:54.449 --> 00:04:58.860
the movie from a comedy-drama into a morality
play, reminding the audience to be grateful

00:04:58.860 --> 00:04:59.700
for what they have.

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:01.340
Shutter Island

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:06.960
Shutter Island, the Martin Scorsese adaptation
of the novel of the same name, is a period

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:12.069
thriller boasting a murderer's row of talent
including Leonardo DiCaprio, Mark Ruffalo,

00:05:12.069 --> 00:05:12.840
and Ben Kingsley.

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:19.120
In 1954, U.S. Marshal Teddy Daniels investigates
an insane asylum following the escape of notorious

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:23.460
murderer Andrew Laeddis while dealing with
the suspicious Dr. Cawley and the disappearance

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:24.540
of his partner, Chuck.

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:28.080
The central twist of the movie drew most of
the audience's attention.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:32.440
Basically, Teddy Daniels is actually Andrew
Laeddis, acting out a fantasy to restore his

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:36.520
memories following the murder of his wife
after she drowned their children years ago.

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:40.980
But it was the enigmatic final line of the
movie that has confused audiences well beyond

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:41.480
the credits.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:47.080
Teddy, seemingly regressed back to his original
delusion that he is a U.S. Marshal, asks Chuck:

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:49.260
"Which would be worse?

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:54.960
To live as a monster or to die a good man?"

00:05:55.420 --> 00:06:00.040
According to Scorsese's psychiatric advisor
on the film, James Gilligan, the line represents

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:04.120
Teddy faking his own regression, choosing
this path to atone for the murder of his wife.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:08.520
The last moments of the film can be interpreted
as if Teddy were actually saying, "I feel

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:10.100
too guilty to go on living.

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:13.889
I'm not going to actually end my life, but
I'm going to vicariously do it by handing

00:06:13.889 --> 00:06:16.600
myself over to these people who are going
to lobotomize me."

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:18.160
Thanks for watching!

00:06:18.169 --> 00:06:20.630
